{
  "dom_id": "step-q86484",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/1fcdf0d68d78cba445d1e3ecfeec237ee4e33d7c74cb3b292593fe8b717fe6b8.png)\n\nPeter notices that the total number of eggs produced on his farm $t$ weeks after the beginning of his observations is given by the function $N(t).$ The graph of $N(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the number of produced eggs increases when $t=5$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{N(t)-N(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=5.$ Applying the above formula with $t=5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{\u2032}(5) & \u2248\\frac{N(5)-N(5-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(5)-N(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{800-600}{1} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of increase is $200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}.$",
